@startuml export-dataflow
' What we achieved: data flow of one frame, with the four wins annotated
' Strict black & white. Render: plantuml -tsvg export-dataflow.puml

title Export Data Flow  ·  ~6 fps → 40+ fps

skinparam monochrome true
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam ArrowColor black
skinparam roundCorner 6
left to right direction

[Source recording\n.webm] as SRC
[Editor state\nzoom · crop · effects] as CFG
[StreamingDecoder] as DEC
[Worker pool\nN PixiJS renderers] as WP
[ffmpeg\nhardware encoder] as FF
[Final video\n.mp4] as OUT

SRC --> DEC : encoded H.264
DEC --> WP : VideoFrame → ImageBitmap\n(transferable)
CFG --> WP : per-frame transform
WP --> FF : raw RGBA\n(TCP, zero-copy)
FF --> OUT : H.264 + audio, muxed

note bottom of OUT
  Four changes closed the gap:
  (1) render N frames in parallel across workers
  (2) read pixels via gl.readPixels, not a 2D canvas
  (3) encode on the GPU (NVENC / QSV / AMF), not in JS
  (4) stream frames over a TCP socket, no per-frame IPC
  ----
  Result:  ~6 fps  →  40+ fps      (13 min  →  ~1 min for a 90 s clip)
end note

@enduml
